WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.140
We’re in a space race with China.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:09.660
NASA Administrator Bill Nelson is warning that&nbsp;
China might start claiming territory on the moon.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:17.520
You see the actions of the Chinese government on&nbsp;
Earth. They go out and claim some international

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.580
islands in the South China Sea, and then&nbsp;
they claim them as theirs. So naturally,

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:34.620
I don’t want China to get to the South Pole first&nbsp;
with humans and then say, ‘This is ours, stay out.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:37.740
That’s the Moon’s south pole he’s talking about.

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:42.240
Spacecraft have detected large amounts&nbsp;
of water ice in the lunar south pole,

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.340
in areas that never receive sunlight.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:49.800
That ice could potentially be extracted and&nbsp;
used for air, drinking water, and rocket fuel.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:54.960
NASA plans to send a rover called VIPER&nbsp;
in 2024 to further explore the area.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.600
VIPER will drill into the Moon's surface,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.040
analyzing samples to create detailed&nbsp;
maps of where water is concentrated.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:07.920
We want to make sure that that’s available&nbsp;
to all, not just the one that’s claiming it.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:12.240
The South Pole’s deep and dark craters&nbsp;
significantly differ from the bright

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.820
and relatively flat surface where Neil&nbsp;
Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed in 1969.

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:20.460
They’ve got the flag up now, you&nbsp;
can see the Stars and Stripes.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.580
It’s been 50 years since the Apollo missions.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.820
We choose to go to the Moon in this decade and

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:31.620
do the other things not because they&nbsp;
are easy but because they are hard.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.700
President Kennedy’s challenge set up&nbsp;
a competition with the Soviet Union,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.920
the other great superpower at the time.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.700
Today, a new competitor has emerged.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.180
China’s space program is notoriously secretive.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.460
While NASA proudly streams live video from the&nbsp;
International Space Station, nobody knew about

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:54.600
the recent spacewalks aboard China’s Tiangong&nbsp;
space station until after they happened.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.620
According to the Economist,&nbsp;
China’s secrecy isn’t surprising.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.960
“Given the prestige at stake, (Chinese)&nbsp;
officials may be inclined to hide failures.”

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.040
Another reason is the close ties&nbsp;
between China’s space program and

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.760
the People’s Liberation Army (PLA) which&nbsp;
demands protection of state interests.

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.420
The U.S. currently leads the “space race”:

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.620
It spent $62 billion on&nbsp;
its space programs in 2022,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:22.740
reportedly five times more than what China spent.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:28.200
The U.S. has 4,500 satellites in orbit&nbsp;
around Earth, while China has 600.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:30.660
But Beijing is catching up fast.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:35.220
It built the Tiangong space station in&nbsp;
just a year and a half compared to the

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:40.200
13 years it took to construct the ISS,&nbsp;
albeit the ISS is five times larger.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.740
The Pentagon predicts that&nbsp;
China will surpass the U.S.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.940
and become the dominant player in space by 2045.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:50.760
China has already made remarkable progress since

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.720
sending its first astronaut - or&nbsp;
taikonaut - into space in 2003.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.800
It became the First Nation to land a&nbsp;
spacecraft on the far side of the moon.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.960
And only the second country to&nbsp;
successfully operate a rover on Mars.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.240
Its next goal is to land astronauts&nbsp;
near the moon’s south pole by 2030.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.200
The question is: Will the U.S. return&nbsp;
to the moon before China gets there?

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.180
Artemis plans to send a crew in&nbsp;
late 2025 but delays are likely.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.300
NASA selected SpaceX’s Starship for the mission,

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:26.640
although the fully reusable rocket hasn’t&nbsp;
yet completed a successful orbital flight.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.520
Starship launched for the first time in April,

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.230
soaring across the Texas sky before&nbsp;
plummeting into the Gulf of Mexico.

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:33.291
NASA associate administrator&nbsp;
Jim Free expressed his doubts.

00:03:33.291 --> 00:03:41.940
"I think my concern is the same because they&nbsp;
haven't launched. They need to launch multiple

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:47.700
times -- not just for us, but for them. And&nbsp;
then they need to launch multiple times for us.”

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:52.080
The journey to the moon requires a modified&nbsp;
Starship to launch into Earth’s orbit.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.500
Another Starship refuels it before&nbsp;
it heads on a path to the moon.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.880
NASA’s SLS will launch from Earth&nbsp;
carrying crew in the Orion spacecraft,

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.400
which meets up with Starship in lunar orbit.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.600
Astronauts will transfer from Orion to&nbsp;
Starship, which will take them to the moon.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.120
Since there's very little atmosphere on the moon,

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:12.360
Starship is modified to do&nbsp;
without fins for landing.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.440
For the trip home, Starship meets&nbsp;
up with Orion left in lunar orbit.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.860
The crew transfers over to&nbsp;
Orion for the return to Earth.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.000
But imagine a scenario where&nbsp;
the SLS and Orion aren’t needed.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.400
Starship could launch with the crew aboard,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.180
rendezvous for refueling with another&nbsp;
Starship, and then head to the moon.

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:33.840
This version of Starship retains&nbsp;
its heat shield and flaps,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.140
ensuring a safe and controlled&nbsp;
descent for landing back on Earth.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.680
China is planning its own version of Starship,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:43.980
the Long March 9, but it's&nbsp;
believed to be a decade away.

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.900
Starship maybe NASA’s pivotal asset in&nbsp;
outpacing China’s progress in space.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:55.680
NASA's relationship with SpaceX is one of its&nbsp;
169 international partnerships, a network far

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.200
more extensive than China's alliances with&nbsp;
Russia, Iran, Pakistan, Thailand, and Turkey.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.340
The possibility of China and the U.S.&nbsp;
working together is off the table.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:09.960
Congress passed the Wolf Amendment in&nbsp;
2011, which prohibits NASA from working

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:13.920
with Chinese government-owned entities&nbsp;
due to fears of technological theft,

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:17.940
risks to national security, and&nbsp;
worries about human rights abuses.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:21.900
So the U.S. and China are on&nbsp;
separate paths toward the moon.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:24.960
Whoever wins the race could gain a considerable

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.760
advantage as the moon is considered to&nbsp;
be the critical stepping stone to Mars.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.640
The race is on, and the stakes are high.

